WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.392
Lúcia maintains the knowledge base chat of a logistics company.

00:00:05.692 --> 00:00:10.829
Hybrid search returns fifty passages for every question, and the model gets the top

00:00:10.829 --> 00:00:11.138
five.

00:00:11.438 --> 00:00:14.133
Then the eval showed something curious.

00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:18.929
In many of the wrong answers, the right passage was among the fifty.

00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:22.098
It just sat at position twelve, or twenty.

00:00:22.398 --> 00:00:23.854
The model never saw it.

00:00:24.504 --> 00:00:29.413
In this lesson, you'll learn to get the best passages to the top, without paying for

00:00:29.413 --> 00:00:31.458
the main model to read all of them.

00:00:32.658 --> 00:00:34.088
Think of two sieves.

00:00:34.388 --> 00:00:39.084
The coarse one is fast, and it separates the gravel from the sand.

00:00:39.384 --> 00:00:41.998
The fine one is slow, and it picks out the gold.

00:00:42.298 --> 00:00:45.468
You never run the fine sieve over the whole riverbed.

00:00:45.768 --> 00:00:47.931
Only over what the first one kept.

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.563
The first stage is retrieval.

00:00:50.863 --> 00:00:53.376
It is fast, and it casts a wide net.

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:58.307
Hybrid or vector search brings back thirty to a hundred candidates.

00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:02.590
The goal is recall: the right one has to be on the list.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.298
The second stage is reranking.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.132
Slow and precise.

00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:12.551
A model reads the question and each candidate together, and gives a relevance score.

00:01:12.851 --> 00:01:17.118
The goal is precision: the top ones have to be the best.

00:01:17.768 --> 00:01:19.767
Why can't the first stage do both?

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:24.732
Because fast search never reads the question and the passage together.

00:01:25.032 --> 00:01:29.099
It compares them separately, as vectors or lists of terms.

00:01:29.399 --> 00:01:34.372
So a passage that talks around the topic can beat one that answers exactly.

00:01:35.572 --> 00:01:36.871
The question arrives.

00:01:37.171 --> 00:01:40.904
How long is the deadline to contest a fleet traffic fine?

00:01:41.204 --> 00:01:46.613
Notice that the words fleet, fine and deadline all appear in many passages.

00:01:47.263 --> 00:01:51.071
A fast search brings back fifty candidates, favoring recall.

00:01:51.371 --> 00:01:53.083
Here is what sits on top.

00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:56.795
Position one talks about the fleet's insurance.

00:01:57.095 --> 00:02:01.530
Position two, about payroll deductions for driver infractions.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.772
Position three, about fuel.

00:02:04.072 --> 00:02:05.280
All on topic.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.804
None of them answers.

00:02:07.454 --> 00:02:10.789
The reranker reads the question together with each passage.

00:02:11.089 --> 00:02:15.898
And the one at position twelve is the only one that says fifteen days.

00:02:16.198 --> 00:02:17.525
It gets the highest score.

00:02:17.825 --> 00:02:23.524
The payroll passage talks about fines, but not about contesting them, and it drops.

00:02:24.174 --> 00:02:28.723
The candidates are sorted by the new score, and only the top five move on.

00:02:29.373 --> 00:02:33.691
And the main model answers from a few passages, all relevant.

00:02:34.341 --> 00:02:35.771
There's one more piece.

00:02:36.071 --> 00:02:37.224
A minimum score.

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:42.207
If none of the five reaches it, the system says it did not find that, instead of

00:02:42.207 --> 00:02:44.490
inventing an answer from the fuel page.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.848
And why not send all fifty to the main model?

00:02:48.148 --> 00:02:52.253
Besides the cost, there's a known effect called lost in the middle.

00:02:52.553 --> 00:02:58.044
Even when everything fits, the model uses what sits in the middle of the context

00:02:58.044 --> 00:02:58.456
worse.

00:02:58.756 --> 00:03:02.315
Fifty mediocre passages are not fifty useful ones.

00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:04.622
Here's one for you.

00:03:04.922 --> 00:03:11.252
The eval says recall at fifty is fifty five percent, and precision at five is fifty

00:03:11.252 --> 00:03:11.862
percent.

00:03:12.162 --> 00:03:14.435
The team installs a reranker.

00:03:14.735 --> 00:03:16.554
What is more likely to happen?

00:03:19.604 --> 00:03:21.992
Precision rises only a little.

00:03:22.292 --> 00:03:27.416
Because on almost half the questions, the right passage isn't even among the fifty.

00:03:27.716 --> 00:03:30.782
A reranker only reorders what arrived.

00:03:31.082 --> 00:03:33.364
It can't create what search didn't bring back.

00:03:34.014 --> 00:03:37.763
The ceiling of precision is the recall of retrieval.

00:03:38.063 --> 00:03:43.399
If recall is low, changing the reranker is changing the stage that already works.

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:47.940
When the right result isn't among the candidates at all.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.302
Fix retrieval first, with hybrid search or query rewriting.

00:03:52.952 --> 00:03:56.877
When latency is very tight and search already gets the top right.

00:03:57.177 --> 00:04:01.892
A search box that must answer in two hundred milliseconds doesn't have room for a

00:04:01.892 --> 00:04:03.872
four hundred millisecond reranker.

00:04:04.522 --> 00:04:07.725
And when the material fits whole in the context.

00:04:08.025 --> 00:04:10.136
Then there's nothing to rerank.

00:04:10.786 --> 00:04:12.071
Now the common traps.

00:04:12.371 --> 00:04:15.499
First, a threshold copied from a tutorial.

00:04:15.799 --> 00:04:19.266
A cross-encoder score isn't a probability.

00:04:19.566 --> 00:04:24.548
Look at the scores of the right passages and of the wrong ones in your eval, and cut

00:04:24.548 --> 00:04:24.904
there.

00:04:25.554 --> 00:04:28.758
Second, reranking only eight candidates.

00:04:29.058 --> 00:04:32.993
If the right result sits at twenty, a short pool leaves it out.

00:04:33.293 --> 00:04:34.724
The pool is recall.

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:37.356
The top few afterwards is precision.

00:04:38.006 --> 00:04:41.233
And third, near-identical passages at the top.

00:04:41.533 --> 00:04:44.884
Three paragraphs of the same policy take three slots.

00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:47.924
After reranking, drop the redundant neighbors.

00:04:49.124 --> 00:04:49.983
What to measure.

00:04:50.283 --> 00:04:53.711
Recall for retrieval, and precision after reranking.

00:04:54.011 --> 00:04:58.965
And the final answer quality, because the goal is the answer, not the search.

00:04:59.615 --> 00:05:02.860
Always keep the arm with search alone, so you can compare.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.713
If the reranker makes precision worse, it's out of domain.

00:05:07.013 --> 00:05:11.212
Logistics vocabulary, acronyms, current documents.

00:05:11.512 --> 00:05:16.977
In that case, a small language model with a written criterion often beats a generic

00:05:16.977 --> 00:05:17.899
encoder there.

00:05:18.549 --> 00:05:22.820
Log a span with the ids before and after, and the scores.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.100
When an answer is wrong, you can see whether the right passage rose and the model

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.375
ignored it, or whether it never rose.

00:05:31.025 --> 00:05:34.621
And alert when the rate of not found answers rises.

00:05:34.921 --> 00:05:38.427
It's the sign of a bad threshold, or of bad retrieval.

00:05:39.077 --> 00:05:40.353
One security point.

00:05:40.653 --> 00:05:44.637
The reranker reads the same untrusted content RAG reads.

00:05:44.937 --> 00:05:50.040
A wiki page that says this document is the most relevant of all can inflate a

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.161
language model reranker's score.

00:05:52.461 --> 00:05:57.191
Cross-encoders suffer less from that rhetoric, but they aren't a defense.

00:05:57.491 --> 00:05:59.486
Permissions belong in retrieval.

00:05:59.786 --> 00:06:03.442
The reranker must never see a passage the user can't see.

00:06:04.642 --> 00:06:06.633
On the page, there are two tabs.

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:09.683
The Python one uses a cross-encoder.

00:06:09.983 --> 00:06:15.282
It asks search for fifty candidates, scores each pair of question and passage, sorts

00:06:15.282 --> 00:06:18.752
by score, and keeps the top five that pass the minimum.

00:06:19.402 --> 00:06:23.552
That's reranking in milliseconds, with no language model call.

00:06:23.852 --> 00:06:27.897
The price is that the encoder doesn't understand business rules.

00:06:28.197 --> 00:06:31.536
A filter like current documents only stays in the search.

00:06:32.186 --> 00:06:35.962
The TypeScript tab swaps the encoder for a language model.

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:41.053
It sends the candidates with their dates, and asks for a score from zero to three,

00:06:41.053 --> 00:06:42.280
in structured output.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:45.426
Superseded versions get at most one.

00:06:45.726 --> 00:06:51.159
That's a criterion you write in plain words, and in exchange you pay one call per

00:06:51.159 --> 00:06:51.763
question.

00:06:52.413 --> 00:06:54.323
Start with the cross-encoder.

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:59.772
Step up to the language model only when relevance depends on a rule the encoder

00:06:59.772 --> 00:07:01.205
never saw in training.

00:07:02.405 --> 00:07:04.595
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:04.895 --> 00:07:08.763
Retrieval is for recall, reranking is for precision.

00:07:09.063 --> 00:07:14.812
Let the cheap stage cast a wide net, let the careful stage read pairs, and send the

00:07:14.812 --> 00:07:16.890
main model only what survived.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:20.859
And measure each stage on its own, so you know which one to fix.

00:07:21.509 --> 00:07:26.521
But what if the right passage never makes the list, because the question was badly

00:07:26.521 --> 00:07:26.949
worded?

00:07:27.249 --> 00:07:32.270
In the next lesson, we'll rewrite the question before searching: query rewriting.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:33.601
See you there.
